// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    David Versus Goliath
// @category Combat Tropes
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/David_Versus_Goliath
// @domain   Interpersonal
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): the hero is vastly outmatched in size, strength, or numbers, yet wins —
// because he is Weak but Skilled against an Unskilled but Strong foe, and by CHANGING HIS TACTICS the
// underdog becomes the favorite. The signature this file recognizes is the INVERSE of a curb-stomp:
// the weaker party does NOT meet force with force (he refuses that contest), he finds and strikes the
// giant's one specific VULNERABILITY by an unconventional approach. Rides the action-dynamics
// Exploit_Weakness -> Finisher chain — the strike is aimed at the foe's kind of weakness, which beats
// any guard and opens them up, and the finisher ends it. Distinct from The Trickster / Feint: those
// ride Bait -> Punish_Overcommit (a deception draws an overcommit); here there is a REAL weakness and
// the underdog's skill, not the foe's mistake, is what lands the blow.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // conflict
import "trl/modules/action_dynamics.trl"   // Exploit_Weakness -> [+Exposed];  Finisher -> [+Defeated]

assoc david_versus_goliath -> [
  action_dynamics : "the Exploit_Weakness / Finisher rules of",
  conflict        : "wins, while outmatched, a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// The underdog victory is built on skill aimed at a weakness, not strength — an inversion of the
// expected rout. (Outmatched is the gating condition; the deed reads as the weak overturning the strong.)
imply DavidVersusGoliath -> [Skill, Combat, Underdog, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: an [+Outmatched] actor whose strike exploits the stronger foe's specific [+Vulnerable]
// weakness, then finishes him, IS a David Versus Goliath (tags the finishing deed). The [+Outmatched]
// precondition on the actor and the [+Vulnerable] precondition on the foe are the signature: the
// weaker party wins by hitting the one weak point, not by matching force. Same Exploit_Weakness ->
// Finisher mechanics as a plain skilled kill, but the SIGNATURE is who wins (the underdog) and how
// (the giant's own vulnerability, struck unconventionally).
rule DavidVersusGoliath {
  when:
    char $david [+Outmatched]
    char $goliath [+Vulnerable(to=$k)] [+Exposed]
    evt  $f [&Finishes(agent=$david, target=$goliath)]
  then:
    $f [+DavidVersusGoliath]
    $david [+Outwits(target=$goliath)]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: David and Goliath (Hebrew scripture, 1 Samuel 17, Iron Age) ===
// The Philistine champion Goliath — a giant in bronze armor — challenges Israel to single combat, and
// every soldier is too afraid to face him. David, a shepherd boy, refuses King Saul's offered armor
// and sword: he will not fight the giant's fight. Instead he keeps to the one skill he has, the sling,
// and meets the giant at range. Goliath's one unguarded spot is his forehead, bare above the bronze;
// David's stone finds it, the giant falls stunned and exposed, and David finishes him with the giant's
// own sword. The eponym of the trope: size and armor undone by skill aimed at the single weak point.

// DAVID (the shepherd)
char david [+Protagonist] [+Outmatched]
david [+Skilled]
// GOLIATH OF GATH (the Philistine champion)
char goliath [+Antagonist]
goliath [+Vulnerable(to="Forehead")]
// THE SLING (the unconventional weapon — range, not reach)
obj sling [+MacGuffin]
// EXT. THE VALLEY OF ELAH - DAY
set elah [+Exterior] [+Day]

scene the_valley {
  beat 1 {
    david @ elah
    goliath @ elah
    david > sling
    // David refuses Saul's armor and meets the giant at range — the unconventional approach
    evt the_stone [&Strikes(agent=david, target=goliath, kind="Forehead")]
    goliath [+Exposed]   // the stone finds the bare forehead; the giant falls stunned — stated literally so coverage re-checks it
  }
  beat 2 {
    // the finish: with the giant down and exposed, David ends it with Goliath's own sword
    evt the_sword [&Finishes(agent=david, target=goliath)]
    // Finisher -> goliath [+Defeated];  DavidVersusGoliath tags the deed, david [+Outwits(goliath)]
  }
}
